  "rubric_items": [
    {
      "id": "DS-R1",
      "reason": "The design uses only existing modules and layers: router, service, repos, dayBounds, client.ts, todayIn, Pagination and the existing appointment index. It adds no new route, store or migration, and it follows the routes/services/repositories layout.",
      "score": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "DS-R2",
      "reason": "The story map covers S-1, S-2 and S-3. Each S-1 AC maps to dayBounds, CANCELLED exclusion, the 0 default and the Q-02 handling. S-2 maps to the doctor_id/date link and S-3 to the unchanged DoctorRepo, Pagination and columns. The Q-02 default now matches the stories' Q-05.",
      "score": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "DS-R3",
      "reason": "The interface is now named and typed: with_today (boolean, default false), today_count (integer, 0 or more), DoctorListRow, countTodayByDoctor(doctorIds, from, to) with its SQL predicate, and the link /appointments?doctor_id=&lt;id&gt;&amp;date=&lt;YYYY-MM-DD&gt;.",
      "score": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "DS-R4",
      "reason": "Facts are cited, and the proposed names, filters, time-zone source and tile rule are asked in Q-01 to Q-05. The picker call is now sourced to impact. One small gap: the web generic Page&lt;T&gt; is used without a cited fact.",
      "score": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "DS-R5",
      "reason": "There are four trade-offs: separate query vs join, opt-in vs always on, list-only type vs shared type, and the status rule. Each gives a reason and a cost, including the possible temporary mismatch with the tile.",
      "score": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "DS-R6",
      "reason": "The flow is clear and numbered. The component and endpoint status in the front matter matches the body. DashboardService and AppointmentsPage now say when they would become changed.",
      "score": 5
    }
  ],
